45,"pass_rate_neutral":0.4792,"def_epa_per_play":-0.3448}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2019,"team":"WAS","distance":1.219,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"DET","distance":1.254,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Anthony Lynn"},{"season":2022,"team":"NYG","distance":1.254,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Mike Kafka"},{"season":2018,"team":"DET","distance":1.285,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"GB","distance":1.285,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Matt LaFleur"}],"defense":[{"season":2018,"team":"CIN","distance":0.5561,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"CLE","distance":0.5965,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"SF","distance":0.6175,"shared":18,"play_caller":"DeMeco Ryans"},{"season":2021,"team":"CIN","distance":0.6214,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"},{"season":2025,"team":"JAX","distance":0.6347,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Anthony Campanile"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Seahawks went 10-6 in 2018 and outscored opponents 428 to 347. The offense ranked 6th in EPA per play and the defense 11th in EPA allowed, so neither side stood out much. What set them apart was the highest 10 personnel usage in the league.","offense":"The Seahawks had one of the most efficient offenses in the league, ranking in the top eight in four of the five main efficiency measures. They were one of the most run-heavy teams in the league, passing less often than game situations would predict. Schematically, they stood out for the highest 10 personnel usage and the fewest tight ends on the field in the league. Russell Wilson ran the passing game, with Doug Baldwin leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Seahawks defense was a little above average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was EPA allowed per rush, where it ranked 7th, and its weakest was explosive play rate allowed, where it ranked 24th. Frank Clark led the team with 13 sacks, and Quinton Jefferson added 3 despite playing only 56% of the snaps. Their scheme was fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature was a Cover 3 rate that ranked 5th highest in the league.","special":"The Seahawks were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 81% of field goals, which was a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the quarterback, about 16% of the money on the books. Their running backs were among the youngest in the league.","players":"Russell Wilson played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,448 yards. Targets were spread around, with Doug Baldwin and Tyler Lockett leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield was a committee led by Chris Carson and Mike Davis. On defense, Frank Clark led the team with 13 sacks and Bradley McDougald picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Brian Schottenheimer called the offense, and the defensive play caller for this season is not in the coaching data. Their offense looked most like the 2019 Commanders and the 2021 Lions under Anthony Lynn. Their defense looked most like the 2018 Bengals and the 2022 Browns.","trends":"2018 is the first season covered here, so there is no earlier year to compare it with. The charts below show how the Seahawks changed over the seasons that followed.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2018. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}